{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '621,1020p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"- Hidden legacy `--task-path` and `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders; they do not replace the board README/checklist workflow and should not be part of the normal contract\n- Spawn returns a tracked `RUN-...` handle; use `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` only after a run exists and the parent needs an explicit synchronization barrier\n- Standalone `spawn wait` and fused `spawn --wait` exit zero only for `completed`; `failed` and `cancelled` runs return non-zero, while PID-aware stale repair prevents orphaned queued/running/paused runs from hanging wait/follow observers\n- Logwork files and system log resources are scoped by task plus `RUN-...`, preserving concurrent same-role run evidence independently\n- Spawn output includes coordinator observation hints for non-blocking monitoring (`watch`, `events --cursor RUN-ID:0`, `wait`) and keeps them runnable in the same executable / board context that produced the run\n- `task-board spawn --json` exposes the same contract in `observation.*`; `observation.inSessionDelivery=true` only when the current Codex orchestrator session was launched through `task-board codex`\n- `observation.runtime` / `observation.delivery` report the orchestrator runtime and its non-blocking completion channel: `codex-session`/`codex-in-session` (app-server push), `claude-session`/`claude-background-wait` (harness background wait wakes the session), or `external`/`external-watch`\n- Spawn run events use replay cursor tokens (`RUN-ID:seq`) so local CLI, TUI, and remote watchers can resume from the same durable position\n- Spawn directives are first-class durable records with acknowledgement state; use `spawn directive` / `spawn directives` instead of ad hoc notes for cancel/pause/restart/reroute intent\n- Goal-bound directive output always includes the full current `active_goal`; each new directive retains its immutable issued `goal_ref`, and legacy records report unknown context rather than guessing\n- Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch/transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet role-handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer verdict branches, and unmet delivery acceptance create a same-revision autonomous successor instead of a terminal failed owner\n- If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, the successor stays non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start retry lease. Status/wait/follow observation reacquires an expired lease and retries the same successor without duplicating ownership; never treat a failed start attempt as loss of the same-revision owner.\n- A role-handoff goal completes only at its immutable role end status with a new or updated task-scoped outcome artifact. Zero exit while parked in `analysis`/`development` or without that evidence is recoverable and queues the same revision again.\n- Required/light delivery acceptance is `done` plus a producer outcome and a later reviewer run with a persisted evidence-backed `accepted` branch. `changes_requested`, Stop-The-Line, and branchless reviewer exits do not count as acceptance. `review=none` still requires `done`, producer outcome evidence, and an orchestrator evidence check after the latest producer run\n- Provider `blocked` and board `blocked` role exits share one evidence gate. Only board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped Stop-The-Line packet may terminate with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; this is never successful delivery. Missing evidence queues the same revision for recovery.\n- Watcher notifications are external observation surfaces by default; `task-board codex` adds runtime-native terminal delivery back into the active Codex thread\n- Claude orchestrator sessions (`CLAUDECODE` env set) have no injection API; the non-blocking channel is the harness itself — run each printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task (`run_in_background`), fan out one background wait per run, and react as each completion wakes the session. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) Pattern E\n- Single-child variant: fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` registers the tracked run identically and blocks in-process to terminal state with `spawn wait` exit semantics — one background Bash call per child; the `run_in_background` wrapper itself remains a caller/harness decision. See [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) \"Fused Spawn And Wait\"\n- Soft directives are cooperative checkpoint signals only. Spawned agents can inspect them through `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID` + `task-board spawn directives \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\"`; do not assume live in-session push\n- Coordinators must not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` in `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers; `--background` already returns a tracked run. The exception is the fused blocking `spawn ... --wait` barrier, which a Claude orchestrator deliberately runs as a harness background task\n- Spawn validates that the board task brief is usable: description and acceptance criteria must be real, not placeholders or one-line fluff\n- Repeated spawn on the same task must be idempotent: existing injected checklist items are not duplicated\n- When auditing spawn docs/help/source, exclude `.temp/**` from repo-wide searches so stale task-scoped worktrees do not look like current contract files\n\n### Review Policy\n\nEvery board element has a `review` policy. Set it at creation with `create(..., review=required|light|none)`, change it with `set_details(ID, review=...)`, and inspect it through the `review` q projection. New elements and existing elements without the field default to `required`.\n\n- `required` keeps the current producer → reviewer → rework/reviewer loop. A producer handoff at `to-review` must be routed to a reviewer child, and only an accepted reviewer verdict reaches `done`.\n- `light` still requires a reviewer child, but reviewer selection should favor a cheaper effort tier that can satisfy the same evidence and quality gates. This is orchestration guidance, not a hard CLI enforcement rule.\n- `none` lets the Orchestrator accept a producer handoff without spawning a reviewer child. After checking the task-specific acceptance criteria, checklist, validation results, and outcome evidence, the Orchestrator may move the element from `to-review` directly to `done` with `set_status`. Missing or inadequate evidence must be routed back to the producer; `none` never means accepting an unevidenced handoff.\n\nThis subsection qualifies unconditional reviewer-routing guidance elsewhere in the workflow: that guidance describes the default `required` policy. Human review is not introduced by any policy value.\n\n### Model Selection Guide\n\n**Agent selection happens before model selection.** Inspect `project_config()` first. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system.exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits an explicit in-set `--agent` or the current in-set affinity. Out-of-set requests fail before side effects. When no canonical system exists, legacy usable `preferred_agent` and then runtime affinity preserve compatibility. Do not hardcode a provider in reusable orchestration instructions.\n\nProject ceilings constrain the already selected provider; they never replace it. Canonical provider-system membership does not require a ceiling, while legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` still requires the corresponding provider ceiling to be usable. After provider selection, the matching `spawn.ceilings.<agent>` policy and optional role override pin or constrain model and, for Codex or Qwen, reasoning effort.\n\n**`model_criterion=equal` exception.** Inspect the effective target agent/role ceiling in `project_config()` first. When it reports `model_criterion=equal`, skip the per-spawn model selection assessment entirely: do not add `Spawn selection assessment` paragraphs to task notes or resources, and do not pass `--selection-rationale` or `--ack-resolved`. Use the exact configured model and the configured Codex or Qwen reasoning effort, when present, as the pinned pair, passing every required model/effort flag explicitly. A single short note naming the pinned pair at the first spawn is allowed; do not repeat it for later spawns.\n\n**Ordered-criterion deliberate selection.** For `less_or_equal` or `greater_or_equal`, write exactly one non-empty rationale line and pass it as `--selection-rationale`. If the request passes through, spawn directly and omit `--ack-resolved`. If policy changes model or effort, the CLI records `(requested_pair, rationale)` in task notes and returns the typed requested-to-resolved checkpoint without creating a run. Deliberately reassess the resolved pair. If it remains fit, re-invoke with exact `--ack-resolved <model>/<effort>` and a fresh rationale line for the resolved pair; after whitespace normalization that text must differ byte-for-byte from every rationale previously recorded for the same task and role. The accepted tuple is `(resolved_pair, fresh_rationale)` and the manifest carries the tuple history. If the resolved pair is inadequate, request another in-bounds pair or escalate the ceiling constraint. Content quality remains the caller's responsibility; the CLI enforces structural pair binding and freshness rather than semantic text analysis.\n\n```bash\n# First ordered request; rationale is recorded when the pair passes through.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-terra at max fits the bounded implementation and validation burden\"\n\n# After a reported adjustment to gpt-5.6-luna/max, reassess and acknowledge that exact pair.\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max --selection-rationale \"gpt-5.6-luna at max remains adequate for this bounded change and its test gates\" --ack-resolved gpt-5.6-luna/max\n```\n\n**Choose explicitly for maximum task quality per token.** Before every board spawn, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost. Then select the strongest suitable pair that can pass the existing quality gates; do not reflexively spend the strongest model or highest supported effort.\n\n| Task type | Agent | Model | --reasoning-effort | Notes |\n|-----------|-------|-------|--------------------|-------|\n| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical work | codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | **max** | Highest recommended board-managed effort; preserve predictable delegation in task-board |\n| Balanced agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | **max** | Strong fit for routine work with meaningful autonomy or validation |\n| Fast, affordable agentic work | codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | **max** | Luna does not support `ultra` |\n| Complex coding and research | codex | `gpt-5.5` | **xhigh** | Does not support `max` or `ultra` |\n| Everyday coding | codex | `gpt-5.4` | **xhigh** | Strong general coding option for narrower scope |\n| Simpler cost-sensitive work | codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | **xhigh** | Use only when bounded scope and clear validation retain the same quality gate |\n| Ultra-fast code iteration | codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | **xhigh** | Speed-oriented coding model |\n| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | claude | `claude-fable-5` | - | Most capable Claude model; `--model` is still required; Claude rejects reasoning effort |\n| Everyday complex Claude work | claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | - | Recommended Claude default for board children |\n| Routine Claude work | claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | - | Efficient for routine, well-scoped tasks |\n| Fast, cheap Claude checks | claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | - | Fastest for quick answers and bounded checks |\n| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | **xhigh** | Recommended registered Qwen Code model; query current context and Team seat/quota metadata |\n| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | **xhigh** | Preview model; use only when the preview tradeoff fits the task |\n\nFor board-managed work, `max` is the highest recommended effort. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible on supported models, but it enables model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning. Prefer task-board's tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, role assignment, dependencies, and reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. Lower-cost pairs are good choices only when they can still pass the same required tests, build/validation, outcome, and review gates.\n\nCurrent Codex values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (UI: Extra high), `max`, and `ultra`. Compatibility is model-specific; query `models(agent=codex)` before launch. Deprecated model IDs may retain legacy `minimal`, but it is not a current recommendation.\n\nCurrent Qwen Code client values are `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`; task-board passes the selected tier through Qwen's stream-JSON initialize control frame. Query `models(agent=qwen)` for registered model IDs, Qwen Code context windows, supported effort values, and current Token Plan Team seat pricing, monthly quotas, frequency-limit semantics, and plan applicability. Qwen ordinary child runs are tracked like Codex/Claude runs, but Qwen goal-bound owner launches are not supported.\n\n**Budget: unlimited by default.** Don't pass `--budget` unless the user explicitly asks to cap spending. Let agents finish their work — a $5 cap on a $3 task means a failed half-done agent + a retry that costs $6 total. Unlimited is cheaper than retries.\n\nThe CLI blocks missing model, missing Codex/Qwen reasoning, reasoning on Claude, and known incompatible model/effort pairs before board lookup or mutation. Unknown explicit model IDs remain forward-compatible and produce a warning; known wrong-agent model IDs also warn.\n\n### Working on a Task (as sub-agent)\n\n1. Estimate + assign + start → `task-board m 'set_estimate(TASK-260218-abc, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=5); assign(TASK-260218-abc, agent=agent-name); set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=development)'`\n2. Work until the task is genuinely complete or objectively blocked. Do not stop with the task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing` just because one local action finished.\n3. Stop and escalate if completion would require a forced fit: document the constraint, failed assumption, viable options, and exact human/product/architecture decision needed. Do not pile on compensating tests, stubs, flags, or priority rules to make an invalid model appear to work.\n4. Update checklist → `task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc, item=1)'`\n5. Save results → `task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Implementation notes\")'`\n6. Ready for review → `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=to-review)'`\n7. Or blocked → only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision; attach the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed, then set `blocked`\n8. Orchestrator confirms producer evidence, spawns a tracked reviewer child (`task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`), waits or monitors the returned `RUN-...`, then routes by the reviewer-child verdict: accepted → `done`; changes requested → the appropriate producer through `to-dev` or `analysis`, followed by a new reviewer; genuine stop-the-line boundary → `blocked` with the full evidence packet\n\nHuman-facing language is part of the contract: a producer ending at `to-review` says \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\", not \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\". Only a reviewer/coordinator may call the board task done, and only after the board status is actually `done`.\n\n### Dependencies\n\nTask/bug dependencies are execution ground truth. An unfinished stored\n`blockedBy` link makes `isBlocked=true`, and status enforcement refuses to start\nthat task or bug. For story/epic containers, only an unfinished explicitly\nstored manual link is a hard block and makes `isBlocked=true`.\n\nNew Task/Bug records also require an explicit estimated value before their\nfirst transition into `development`. Query `{ estimate }` to inspect the nested\nenum and use `set_estimate(...)` to change it. An absent Estimate section is a\nlegacy persistence state and does not activate the gate retroactively.\nStory and Epic estimates are never persisted or mutated. Their `{ estimate }`\nprojection rolls up descendant Task/Bug values to `estimated(fibonacci(sum))`,\nor to `partiallyEstimated` with the known `rolledEstimate`, an `N/total`\n`notEstimatedCount`, and sorted `unestimatedTaskIds`.\n\nCross-container waiting derived from the live task DAG is advisory scheduling\nstate: it is never persisted, never changes `isBlocked`, and never adds a\ncontainer-level status-enforcement rule. Query projections expose active\nadvisory waiting as `isAdvisoryBlocked=true` and identify its source through\n`derivedBlockedBy`. `summary()` and `list(blocked=true)` report both hard and\nadvisory entries and annotate each with `isBlocked` and\n`isAdvisoryBlocked`; a mixed container has both flags set. Use these annotations\nbefore deciding whether an orchestrator is seeing manual intent or computed\nordering.\n\n```bash\n# TASK-B depends on TASK-A\ntask-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# CLI will refuse to move TASK-B to development while TASK-A is not done\ntask-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-def, status=development)'\n# Error: cannot start TASK-260218-def — blocked by: TASK-260218-abc\n\n# Remove dependency\ntask-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'\n\n# Inspect hard and advisory scheduling constraints together\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(blocked=true) { id isBlocked isAdvisoryBlocked blockedBy derivedBlockedBy }'\n```\n\n### Board Review\n\n```bash\n# Queries (use --format compact for token efficiency)\ntask-board q 'schema()'                                                          # discover fields, presets, operations\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                  # confirm runtime, launch gate, and model criterion before agent work\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'                                        # quick overview\ntask-board q --format compact 'get(TASK-260218-abc) { full }'                    # full element details\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'  # what's in progress\ntask-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, blocked=true) { id name status }'  # what's blocked\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'                                         # agent dashboard\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'             # execution waves (super-compact planning shape)\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-260218-abc, TASK-260218-def], mode=children)'     # plan over a set of subtrees (union)\n\n# Full-text search (local only)\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"\n\n# Validation (local only)\ntask-board validate\ntask-board repair-links\n```\n\n---\n\n## Querying & Mutating the Board\n\nThree primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\n\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"` (local only)\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\n\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\n\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\n\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\n\n---\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \"works\" is bad, \"test passes\" is good\n- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer\n- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task\n- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story\n- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope\n- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done\n- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review\n- **Rendering is human visualization on explicit request only** — agents never render or open graphs to derive waves or the critical path. The query model already provides that data; see [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) for the centralized render examples\n- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt\n- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`\n- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID\n- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks\n- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.\n- **Spec decomposition stays inline** — creating/refining the board hierarchy from a settled spec is the Orchestrator's own work. Never create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely for board layout; use that role only when a genuine architecture decision must be made first.\n- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.\n- **All specialist deliverable work through tracked background spawn** — every specialist board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also require explicit `--reasoning-effort`. Inline settled-spec board layout is the Orchestrator's explicit exception. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for specialist deliverables.\n- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.\n- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\". Coordinators must not tell the human \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.\n- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion\n- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\n- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.\n- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also need a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices\n- **Provider system, then ceilings** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`; `exclusive` pins, `mixed` uses an explicit in-set `--agent` or in-set affinity, and out-of-set providers are refused before side effects. Legacy `preferred_agent` keeps its best-effort ceiling/executable gate. The selected provider's ceiling then resolves model/reasoning.\n- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect\n- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete --confirm` to remove one\n- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL\n- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME --confirm`\n- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`\n- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`\n- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template\n- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files\n- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)\n- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)\n- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store\n\n---\n\n## Remote Board Mode\n\nThe board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.\n\n### Setup\n\n```bash\n# Start board-server\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server\ngo run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090\n\n# Set environment (token from first-run output)\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090\nexport TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\n\n# Create a board\ncurl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN\" \\\n  -d '{\"id\":\"my-project\",\"name\":\"My Project\"}' \\\n  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards\n\n# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'\n```\n\n### How It Works\n\n- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP\n- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board\n- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side\n- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization\n- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments\n- [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first\n- `grep` and `validate` are local-only; `tree` and `task-board plan --render` can present data fetched from either a local or remote board\n- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally\n- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode\n\n### Config Resolution\n\nPriority:\n- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env\n- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`\n- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)\n- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`, optional `spawn`) — **no token or active user in config file**\n- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)\n- top-level `version_control.confirm` defaults to `false`; when enabled, `desired_commit_time` is required and Bug/Story transitions to `done` require `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This includes automatic Story promotion caused by its last Task/Bug becoming terminal. The acknowledgement decision and the child/ancestor status writes share one serialized mutation boundary in local and remote mode. Before sending an enforcing remote mutation, the CLI requires `GET /v1/version` to advertise `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1`, then verifies the response acknowledgement too. The acknowledgement applies only to the mover's scope; no git clean-tree check is performed.\n- `project_config().explicit_remote_override` is true only for remote flag/environment overrides; a remote profile from `task-board.config.json` alone reports false.\n- The CLI rejects a resolved remote context combined with an explicit local selector: `--board-dir`, `TASK_BOARD_DIR`, or `TASK_BOARD_MODE=local`.\n- CLI and TUI startup parse the sidecar per section and emit non-fatal stderr diagnostics for malformed, unknown, and legacy sections. Every diagnostic names the exact dotted section and expected shape; valid sibling sections still select the board. `--json` uses newline-delimited warning objects on stderr while command data stays on stdout. Policy consumers remain strict, so startup tolerance never authorizes an operation that depends on an invalid section.\n- Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains operational but is diagnosed with the canonical `preferred_agentic_system` shape. The abandoned top-level `agents` policy, plaintext `remote.token`, unknown keys, and the misspelled `preferred_agent_system` are also reported instead of being silently dropped or migrated.\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd\n- Spawn policy is profile-aware: local mode reads root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both support `enabled`, `max_parallel`, canonical `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, agent ceilings, and role overrides with identical preflight enforcement. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the active key path, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports the base system, windows, and active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field; `max_parallel` controls only the client's local workdir queue, not remote-board-wide concurrency.\n\nToken resolution (in order):\n1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env\n2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)\n3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`\n\nKeychain-first auth model:\n- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`\n- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)\n- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`\n- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path\n- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow\n\nGlobal non-versioned auth state file:\n- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`\n- shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"remotes\": {\n    \"https://board.example.com\": {\n      \"active_user\": \"admin\",\n      \"active_users\": {\n        \"my-project\": \"alexis\",\n        \"ops-project\": \"admin\"\n      },\n      \"users\": {}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote\n- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists\n- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here\n\nMigration from legacy plaintext credentials:\n```bash\ntask-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup\ntask-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file\n```\n\nOther:\n- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory\n- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)\n- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)\n\n### Board Migration Workflows\n\nArchive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.\n\nWhat is preserved:\n- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)\n- statuses\n- assignee\n- original element update timestamps\n- notes/checklists/dependencies\n- precondition/outcome resource refs\n- resource payload files byte-for-byte\n\nEach export carries a versioned transfer manifest. Import validates the full\narchive before mutation, rejects unsafe/duplicate paths and target ID\ncollisions, and rolls back all writes if applying the archive fails. Remote\nimports are compressed-size bounded by the server (64 MiB by default).\n\nCore commands:\n```bash\n# Export current board to archive\ntask-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz\n\n# Import archive into current board\ntask-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Local -> Remote\n\n```bash\n# source project (local mode)\ncd ~/src/source-project\ntask-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n\n# remote-connected project\ncd ~/src/target-project\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Remote -> Local\n\n```bash\n# remote-connected project\ncd ~/src/source-remote-project\ntask-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\n\n# target local project\ncd ~/src/target-local-project\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \"mode\": \"local\",\n  \"local\": {\n    \"board_dir\": \".task-board\"\n  },\n  \"spawn\": {\n    \"max_parallel\": 20\n  }\n}\nJSON\n\ntask-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Round-trip Before Deploy\n\nUse a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management\nmake install-backend\n\n# 2. Start local board-server\nboard-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db\n\n# 3. Project-local remote config\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \"mode\": \"remote\",\n  \"remote\": {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1021,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1021,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"    \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:8091\",\n    \"board\": \"roundtrip-playground\",\n    \"tls_no_verify\": false,\n    \"spawn\": {\n      \"max_parallel\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\nJSON\n\n# 4. Store token, create board, import archive\ntask-board login admin tb_xxxxx\ntask-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground \"Roundtrip Playground\"\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n\n# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board\ntask-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz\n```\n\nFor local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.\n\n---\n\n## Setup & Deployment\n\nTwo deployment modes. Choose based on your use case:\n\n| | Local | Service |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Board storage** | `.task-board/` directory in project | board-server (bbolt + MinIO) |\n| **Multi-agent** | Single agent at a time | Multiple agents concurrently |\n| **Persistence** | Git (committed with project) | Server-side (backed up separately) |\n| **Network** | None | HTTP (LAN or remote) |\n| **Setup time** | Zero — just use CLI | 2 min (make) or 5 min (Docker) |\n| **Best for** | Solo dev, small projects | Teams, swarm agents, persistent boards |\n\n### Local mode (default)\n\nNo board bootstrap needed. CLI creates `.task-board/` on first `create` command.\nIf you run setup/install scripts, profile configs are created automatically:\n\n```bash\n# Canonical workstation setup for CLI/TUI + installed skill artifact\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode local --board-dir .task-board\n\n# Start using\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\n```\n\nBoard lives in `.task-board/` inside your project. Commit it to git for persistence.\n\nIf this board lives inside a real code repository that agents will work on, bootstrap it from the `skill-project-management` source checkout with repo-local agents enabled:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\nThis is the expected companion step for board setup in a project repo:\n- `.agents/` gets the local runtime contents and templates\n- `.claude/` and `.codex/` are refreshed as thin links into `.agents/`\n- spawned agents can reliably see repo-local instructions instead of only global installs\n- agents can call `task-board q 'project_config()'` before work to verify whether this repo uses a local board or a remote profile\n\n### Service mode (board-server)\n\n#### Quick start (local binary)\n\n```bash\n# Install CLI/TUI + project profile via the canonical setup path\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\n\n# If this board drives work in a code repo, prefer the single setup command with repo-local agents\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-board my-project --bootstrap-local-agents\n\n# Build board-server binary (binary-only helper)\nmake install-backend\n\n# Start server locally\nboard-server --addr :8080 --data-dir ./data/boards --auth-db ./data/auth.db\n```\n\nServer prints everything you need on startup:\n```\n  board-server running:\n  URL:         http://localhost:8080\n  Data:        ./data/boards\n\n  FIRST RUN — admin token (save it, shown once):\n  tb_xxxxx\n\n  Quick start:\n    export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\n    export TASK_BOARD_ID=<board-id>\n```\n\nSelect a user, store the token in Keychain, then go:\n```bash\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\n\n# Create a board\ntask-board admin board create my-project \"My Project\"\n\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\n\n# Use normally — all DSL commands route to server\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system)'\n\n# Optional: write remote profile config explicitly\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\n```\n\n#### Docker deployment (production)\n\nFull stack: Caddy (TLS reverse proxy) + board-server + MinIO (resource storage).\n\n```bash\ncd tools/board-server/deploy\ncp .env.example .env\n# Edit .env: set BOARD_DOMAIN, MinIO credentials\n\n./deploy.sh         # Build + start (prints admin token on first run)\n./status.sh         # Check health\n./logs.sh           # Tail logs\n./backup.sh         # Backup data\n./stop.sh           # Stop all\n./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\n```\n\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\n```bash\n# CLI flag\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\n\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\n\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\n{\n  \"mode\":\"remote\",\n  \"local\":{\"board_dir\":\".task-board\"},\n  \"remote\":{\n    \"url\":\"https://board.local\",\n    \"board\":\"my-project\",\n    \"tls_no_verify\":true,\n    \"spawn\":{\"enabled\":true,\"max_parallel\":20}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### User management (service mode)\n\n```bash\n# Create user + token\ntask-board admin user create alice\ntask-board admin token create alice\n\n# Grant board access\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\n\n# List users\ntask-board admin user list\n```\n\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\n\n### Makefile reference\n\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\n\n```bash\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\nmake install            # all three binaries\nmake test               # run all tests\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\n```\n\n---\n---\n\n# Part 2: Planning\n\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\n\nCanonical planning API:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\n```\n\nScope forms:\n\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\n\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\n\n```\nactive:false\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\nelements:4\nmode:children\nscope:EPIC-01\nstatuses:{\"TASK-01\":\"done\",\"TASK-02\":\"development\",\"TASK-03\":\"backlog\",\"TASK-04\":\"backlog\"}\nwaves:[[\"TASK-01\",\"TASK-03\"],[\"TASK-02\"],[\"TASK-04\"]]\n```\n\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\n\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\n\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\n\n```bash\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\n```\n\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\n\n---\n\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\n\n## Assigning Agents\n\n```bash\n# Assign agent to element\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\n\n# Remove assignment\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\n```\n\n## Agent Dashboard\n\n```bash\n# Show active agents\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\n\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\n```\n\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\n\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\n\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\n   ```\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\n\n## Agent Roles\n\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\n\n### Role Registry\n\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\n\n```\n.roles/\n├── developer/\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\n├── researcher/\n│   ├── role.md\n│   └── skills.md\n├── tester/\n│   └── role.md\n└── ...\n```\n\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: developer\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\n  - Code written per task description and AC\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n    classes: [code]\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n    classes: [code]\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\n---\n\n# developer\n\n## Description\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Standing Orders\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\n\n## Deliverable\nCode + tests.\n...\n```\n\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\n\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\n\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\n```markdown\n## Required\n- project-management\n- go-tui-testing-tools\n\n## Domain\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\n- swiftui (when building UI)\n```\n\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\n\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\n\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\n\n**Available roles:**\n\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\n\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\n\nThe spawn pipeline:\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\n\n```bash\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n```\n\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\n\nFor coordinator orchestration:\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\n\n### Skill Injection\n\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\n\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\n\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\n\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\n\nKey points covered there:\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\n\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\n\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\n\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\n\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\n\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\n\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\n- task description comes from the element README\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\n\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\n\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\n\nThe prompt also includes:\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\n\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\n\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\n\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\n\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\n\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\n\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\n\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
