{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'SECURITY.md' | sed -n '1,125p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Security policy\n     2\t\n     3\t## Reporting\n     4\t\n     5\tReport suspected vulnerabilities privately to `ivan@relux.works`. Include the\n     6\taffected protocol section or schema, a minimal reproducer, and the security\n     7\timpact. Do not include secrets, private registry records, or production keys.\n     8\t\n     9\tAcknowledgement is targeted within three business days. Coordinated disclosure\n    10\tand release timing depend on severity and whether deployed implementations need\n    11\tupdates before publication.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Security model\n    14\t\n    15\tThe protocol treats skill repositories, manifests, registry responses, cache\n    16\tentries, and bundles as untrusted input. Conforming implementations MUST apply\n    17\tthe parsing limits and validation rules in the normative documents before\n    18\tusing paths, signatures, references, or configuration values.\n    19\t\n    20\tRepository control includes source, both manifest spellings, a package-provided\n    21\troot `.csk-install.json`, `go.mod`, `go.sum`, `vendor/`, build constraints,\n    22\tcompiler directives, embedded inputs, assembly, host objects, filenames, and\n    23\tfile sizes. Build receipts, artifacts, stale markers, and candidate cache bytes\n    24\tare also untrusted parser and hash input until admitted through the protected\n    25\tcache boundary below.\n    26\t\n    27\tThe protocol provides integrity, provenance, revocation, rollback detection,\n    28\tand deterministic installation. Capability declarations and source auditing\n    29\tare review and policy surfaces; they are not runtime sandboxes. A successful\n    30\taudit or registry attestation does not make skill-provided code safe to execute\n    31\twithout the consuming agent's own isolation and authorization controls.\n    32\t\n    33\t## Compile-only build boundary\n    34\t\n    35\tManifest schema 6 does not introduce package hooks. It admits only untrusted Go\n    36\tsource as compiler input to the closed, manager-owned `go-v1` process described\n    37\tin `protocol/core.md`. During install, update, repair, status, garbage\n    38\tcollection, and dry-run, a conforming manager:\n    39\t\n    40\t- MUST NOT invoke a package-provided executable, script, shell command,\n    41\t  interpreter entry point, shared library, test, package-manager hook, build\n    42\t  backend, or arbitrary build recipe;\n    43\t- MUST NOT accept or derive package-controlled executable names, argument\n    44\t  arrays, environment variables, flags, tags, linker/compiler options,\n    45\t  toolchains, output names or paths, build scripts, pre/post-build actions, or\n    46\t  output-selection rules;\n    47\t- MUST NOT load package-produced code as a compiler or manager plugin, macro,\n    48\t  annotation processor, source generator, loader, or procedural extension;\n    49\t- MUST NOT use `sh -c`, `cmd.exe /c`, PowerShell, or any other shell to create a\n    50\t  build command;\n    51\t- MUST NOT run `go generate`, `go run`, `go test`, `go install`, `go get`, any\n    52\t  `go mod` mutation/download command, or a package-selected VCS/network helper;\n    53\t- MUST NOT invoke or fall back to cgo, an external compiler/linker, libgcc\n    54\t  discovery, package-controlled assembly, host objects, or a dynamic import\n    55\t  requested by `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` in active non-standard Go source;\n    56\t- MUST NOT execute the built output for version discovery, smoke testing,\n    57\t  post-processing, receipt generation, rollback, validation, or any other\n    58\t  install-time purpose; and\n    59\t- MAY perform fixed parsing, hashing, copying, permission changes, and schema\n    60\t  validation in manager code, and MAY pass validated untrusted source bytes to\n    61\t  the exact operator-trusted `go-v1` compiler pipeline.\n    62\t\n    63\tThe manager therefore MUST NOT invoke package Make, CMake, Ninja, or Meson\n    64\trecipes; Cargo build scripts or procedural macros; SwiftPM manifests, plugins,\n    65\tor macros; Maven or Gradle plugins/tasks; MSBuild tasks or Roslyn generators;\n    66\tnpm lifecycle scripts, Node bundler configuration, loaders, or plugins; Python\n    67\tPEP 517 build backends; or comparable executable build-system surfaces. There\n    68\tis no generic-hook fallback when a package cannot fit `go-v1`. A future driver\n    69\trequires a new closed identifier and independent security review.\n    70\t\n    71\t`go-v1` forces internal linking and disables libgcc lookup, cgo, PGO,\n    72\tworkspaces, toolchain switching, non-standard assembly, host objects, and the\n    73\tactive non-standard `//go:cgo_import_dynamic` directive. If the native target\n    74\tor dependency graph cannot build within that surface, installation fails. A\n    75\tmanager MUST reject a build when it cannot enforce the fixed environment,\n    76\tnetwork denial, source/context separation, native target, and process graph;\n    77\tit MUST NOT approximate the contract.\n    78\t\n    79\t### Compiler-input trust boundary\n    80\t\n    81\tCompile-only does not mean trusted source or an invulnerable compiler. Malicious\n    82\tsource may exploit parser/compiler defects or exhaust CPU, memory, disk,\n    83\tprocesses, diagnostic output, or filesystem traversal. The selected Go\n    84\ttoolchain, manager, operating system, hashing/canonicalization code, and\n    85\tmanager-owned policy/staging roots are trusted; package source and compiler\n    86\tdiagnostics are not. Source is read-only to children, standard input is closed,\n    87\tand stdout/stderr MUST be bounded and redacted before presentation. Managers\n    88\tMUST apply filesystem and network denial plus time, memory, disk, process-count,\n    89\tand output limits where the host supports them. The compiled artifact remains\n    90\tuntrusted package code until a user later invokes the installed command under\n    91\tthe consuming environment's authorization and isolation policy.\n    92\t\n    93\t### Protected-cache trust boundary\n    94\t\n    95\tReceipt and marker SHA-256 values establish deterministic consistency and\n    96\tcurrentness; they are not signatures, MACs, registry attestations, or\n    97\tindependent proof that `go-v1` produced an artifact. Persistent reuse is\n    98\tpermitted only inside manager-created state whose ownership, private\n    99\tpermissions or DACL, containment, file type, and link safety are revalidated\n   100\ton every lookup and before locked publication/commit. A self-consistent receipt\n   101\toutside that boundary is attacker-controlled and MUST be treated as a miss.\n   102\tManagers that cannot enforce the boundary MUST disable persistent reuse and\n   103\trebuild from the revalidated snapshot; they MUST NOT repair permissions and\n   104\tadopt pre-existing candidate bytes.\n   105\t\n   106\tThe v1 boundary assumes an adversary cannot mutate protected state as the\n   107\tmanager's operating-system principal or as a trusted administrator. Arbitrary\n   108\tsame-principal code execution, administrator/root or kernel compromise, and\n   109\thostile storage below the operating system are outside this install-time\n   110\tinvariant because they can replace the manager and markers as well as receipts.\n   111\tDeployments that cannot accept that assumption MUST disable persistent cache\n   112\treuse. Authenticated cross-principal artifact provenance would require a future\n   113\tprotocol; a plain receipt hash does not provide it.\n   114\t\n   115\tClient snapshot high-water state is security state. It is stored separately\n   116\tfrom disposable registry responses, written atomically before acceptance, and\n   117\tincluded in protected machine backups. Existing corruption or a failed write\n   118\tis fail-closed. Loss of all local state still requires an out-of-band\n   119\tauthenticated checkpoint or explicit operator rebootstrap; signatures alone\n   120\tcannot prove that a newly presented snapshot is not an old valid view.\n   121\t\n   122\tRegistry trust anchors are distributed out of band. Removing a key from the\n   123\tpinned set revokes trust in signatures made solely by that key. Key rotation\n   124\tand incident behavior are defined in `protocol/registry.md`. The production\n   125\tregistry threat model, including replay, rollback, equivocation, cursor abuse,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'decisions/0004-compile-only-build-drivers.md' | sed -n '1,175p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'decisions/0004-compile-only-build-drivers.md' | sed -n '1,175p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Decision 0004: compile-only build drivers\n     2\t\n     3\t## Context\n     4\t\n     5\tSchemas 1 through 5 can copy script commands or resolve system commands but\n     6\tcannot produce a native artifact from source. Allowing a package to supply a\n     7\tshell command, executable, argument vector, environment, plugin, generator, or\n     8\tbuild-system recipe would contradict the existing rule that installation does\n     9\tnot execute package-provided code.\n    10\t\n    11\tCompiled artifacts also cannot reuse the existing `skill + commit` runtime\n    12\tidentity. Compiler-visible inputs include files outside one package directory,\n    13\tthe selected native target and policy, and the exact trusted toolchain. An\n    14\tinternally consistent receipt from an attacker-writable cache is not proof that\n    15\tthe manager produced its artifact.\n    16\t\n    17\t## Decision\n    18\t\n    19\tManifest schema 6 is a declarative compiled-artifact extension. It adds\n    20\texplicit `build_roots` and one closed command object containing only\n    21\t`type: \"build\"`, `driver: \"go-v1\"`, and `source_dir`. Build roots are dedicated\n    22\tlink-free module roots below the snapshot, cannot be `.`, cannot overlap\n    23\truntime roots, and are statically excluded from agent context and runtime\n    24\tcopying before cache lookup or compiler discovery. The same exclusion applies\n    25\tto real builds, exact cache hits, and dry-runs. Artifacts live only in\n    26\tmanager-owned staging and immutable cache state.\n    27\t\n    28\tProtocol v1 defines only `go-v1`. It is a fixed native, vendor-only,\n    29\tnetworkless, cgo-free Go 1.23+ pipeline using an operator-trusted and\n    30\tfingerprinted release family. The manager owns the process graph, environment,\n    31\targuments, target, output path, dependency preflight, directive checks, and\n    32\tinternal-link policy. It rejects non-standard assembly, host objects,\n    33\t`//go:cgo_import_dynamic`, external linking, libgcc fallback, PGO, workspaces,\n    34\ttoolchain switching, and any executable child outside the fingerprinted Go\n    35\ttool directory. The built artifact is never executed during manager activity.\n    36\t\n    37\tTwo source/currentness identities remain separate:\n    38\t\n    39\t- installed-tree `content_sha256` retains its schema 1 through 5 semantics and\n    40\t  excludes the manager marker;\n    41\t- `curator-build-source-v1` is domain-separated and length-framed over every\n    42\t  regular file in the validated raw snapshot, including a package-provided\n    43\t  root `.csk-install.json`, and is computed before cache lookup.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe build input also binds `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, command/source selection,\n    46\tnative target and tuning, driver revision, and the complete fixed policy. Its\n    47\tCCJ-1 digest is the logical cache key. Exact canonical receipt bytes bind the\n    48\tinput and manager-derived artifact path/hash/size; their digest supplies\n    49\tcorruption and currentness detection, not authentication. Persistent reuse\n    50\trequires a separately verified manager-created, owner-protected, link-safe\n    51\tcache boundary. Untrusted protection forces a rebuild from the revalidated\n    52\tsnapshot, even when every embedded hash matches.\n    53\t\n    54\tMarker schema 2 records build roots, build-source identity, logical cache keys,\n    55\treceipt hashes, artifact hashes, and manager-derived artifact-relative paths.\n    56\tManagers read marker schemas 1 and 2, write v2 after mutation, and may retain a\n    57\tvalid v1 marker as current for schema 1 through 5 packages. Shims for build\n    58\tcommands select immutable cache artifacts rather than the commit-keyed script\n    59\truntime store.\n    60\t\n    61\tA real operation validates and builds misses in operation-private staging\n    62\tbefore installation mutation. Independent projects may validate and compile\n    63\tconcurrently, but cache publication, project/global/hybrid/adapter mutation,\n    64\tconsumer update, rollback/recovery, and garbage collection serialize beneath\n    65\tone manager-home mutation lock. One journal covers the ordered mutable target\n    66\tset; the consumer ledger commits last; rollback stays locked and proceeds in\n    67\treverse commit order. This prevents cross-project lost updates and stale\n    68\tbackup restoration.\n    69\t\n    70\tDry-run performs only package-independent toolchain probes and read-only\n    71\tplanning. It does not run `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or linker; create\n    72\tGo caches, persistent fingerprint memos, mutation locks, journals, or manager\n    73\tstate; publish/quarantine/repair cache entries; or change agent context,\n    74\truntime, markers, shims, adapters, audit/registry state, or consumers.\n    75\t\n    76\tOnly logical identity and validation are portable. Manager-home directories,\n    77\tphysical cache-root and driver-subdirectory names, receipt filenames, lock and\n    78\tquarantine names, and storage backends are deliberately non-normative. An\n    79\tillustrative layout such as `<home>/<cache>/go-v1/<key>/bin/<command>` MUST NOT\n    80\tbe treated as a compatibility identifier.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Rejected alternatives\n    83\t\n    84\t- Arbitrary package shell commands, executable names, argv/environment arrays,\n    85\t  output selection, hooks, plugins, generators, tests, and pre/post-build steps\n    86\t  were rejected because they transfer install-time execution control to the\n    87\t  package.\n    88\t- Cargo was rejected for v1 because build scripts and procedural macros execute\n    89\t  package-selected code. Direct Rust compilation still needs a closed\n    90\t  dependency and linking contract.\n    91\t- Zig and Swift were deferred because their build manifests, plugins, macros,\n    92\t  or compile-time execution require distinct process and side-effect models.\n    93\t- Make, CMake, Ninja, and Meson were rejected as package-controlled recipe\n    94\t  frontends rather than fixed compilers.\n    95\t- Maven, Gradle, MSBuild, and package-oriented Java/Kotlin/.NET compilation were\n    96\t  deferred because tasks, plugins, annotation processors, analyzers, and source\n    97\t  generators execute at build time.\n    98\t- npm lifecycle scripts and Node/TypeScript bundler configuration, loaders, and\n    99\t  plugins were rejected. A fixed transpiler or Deno subset still needs a closed\n   100\t  dependency, configuration, and runtime-output model.\n   101\t- Python package builds were rejected because PEP 517 frontends execute the\n   102\t  package-selected backend and may resolve dynamic build requirements.\n   103\t- Direct C/C++ compilers were deferred because a portable driver must own and\n   104\t  identify source enumeration, preprocessing, sysroots, response files,\n   105\t  compiler plugins, native dependencies, and linking inputs.\n   106\t- `skill + commit`, installed-tree `content_sha256`, and receipt-only trust were\n   107\t  rejected as cache identities because they omit compiler/toolchain policy or\n   108\t  allow attacker-created self-consistent candidates.\n   109\t- Standardizing cache directory names and receipt filenames was rejected\n   110\t  because machine-local layout is an implementation boundary, not wire state.\n   111\t\n   112\t## Compatibility impact\n   113\t\n   114\tSchema 1 through 5 manifests and marker-v1 currentness retain their exact\n   115\tdeployed behavior. They reject the schema-6-only field and command surface.\n   116\tSchema 6 and marker v2 are explicit reader/writer version transitions; readers\n   117\tnever infer them from fields. The build feature is released with a new current\n   118\tprotocol `1.0.0-rc.4` conformance claim schema 2. Conformance claim schema 1\n   119\tremains frozen as historical rc.3 evidence and cannot claim schema-6 or\n   120\tbuild-driver behavior.\n   121\t\n   122\t## Security impact\n   123\t\n   124\tThe accepted model preserves the no-hooks invariant while admitting untrusted\n   125\tcompiler input. It does not claim that the Go compiler or compiled artifact is\n   126\tsafe. Compiler-input denial of service and vulnerabilities require host\n   127\tresource limits and sandboxing, while artifact execution remains a later user\n   128\taction. Protected local cache state is part of the v1 trusted computing base;\n   129\tsame-principal, administrator/root, kernel, and hostile-storage compromise are\n   130\toutside that boundary and require cache reuse to be disabled when unacceptable.\n   131\t\n   132\t## Future-driver rule\n   133\t\n   134\tA future driver requires a new closed identifier and independent protocol and\n   135\tsecurity review. The proposal must define a strict manifest surface, complete\n   136\tcompiler-input boundary, trusted toolchain identity, fixed direct process graph\n   137\tand environment, network/dependency/link policy, manager-derived output,\n   138\tlogical cache/receipt identity, dry-run behavior, lifecycle/rollback rules, and\n   139\tcross-language conformance vectors. It MUST demonstrate that package-selected\n   140\thooks, plugins, generators, macros, build recipes, and produced output cannot\n   141\texecute during manager activity. No future driver may be enabled by broadening\n   142\t`go-v1` or adding a generic fallback.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
