{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,2200p' '/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/planning-workflow.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Planning Workflow\n\nReference document for the full planning cycle: story detailing, phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, execution, supervision, and optional human visualization on explicit request.\n\n---\n\n## Planning Storage: `.planning/`\n\nAll planning artifacts go to the **`.planning/` directory** in the project root. This is the persistent planning archive — always under version control, always accessible between sessions.\n\n```\n.planning/\n├── 260211_143022_spawn-orchestration.md         # YYMMDD_HHmmss format\n├── 260210_091500_agentquery-migration.md\n├── 260209_160345_tui-schema-page.md\n└── 260205_120000_initial-board-setup.md\n```\n\n**Naming convention:** `{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{kebab-case-topic}.md`\n\nWhere:\n- `YYMMDD` — date (year, month, day)\n- `HHmmss` — time (hours, minutes, seconds)\n- Topic — short description of what was planned\n\n### When to Dump Planning\n\n**After phase planning is complete** (before asking user for approval), dump the plan to `.planning/`:\n\n```bash\n# Generate the canonical planning model\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-XX, mode=children)'\n\n# Save a human-readable snapshot of that model to .planning/ with timestamp\ntask-board plan EPIC-XX --save\n```\n\nThe query result is the planning source. Saving a snapshot is only persistence for session recovery; it does not replace `q plan()` as the source of waves, phases, or the critical path.\n\n### Planning Doc Content\n\nEach planning doc should include:\n\n1. **Epic/scope reference** — which epic or scope this plan covers\n2. **Phase breakdown** — phases with stories and tasks per phase\n3. **Dependencies** — critical cross-story dependencies\n4. **Estimated scope** — number of stories, tasks, phases\n5. **Agent allocation** — which agents handle which stories (if known)\n6. **Critical path** — the longest dependency chain\n7. **Risks / open questions** — anything that might block execution\n\n### Why .planning/ Matters\n\n- **Session recovery** — if context dies, the plan is still there\n- **History** — timestamps show planning evolution over time\n- **Reference** — orchestrator can re-read the plan mid-execution\n- **Audit trail** — what was planned vs what was actually built\n\n---\n\n## Story Detailing (MANDATORY before phase planning)\n\n- **The Orchestrator details settled scope inline:** read each Story, create its atomic Tasks/Bugs, write complete descriptions and acceptance criteria, and link dependencies directly through `task-board`. Do not create a decomposition meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to copy settled requirements into the board.\n- Set the Story to `analysis` while its board shape is being refined: `task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc, status=analysis)'`.\n- Read and refine it until the description, scope, AC, leaves, and dependency edges are unambiguous:\n  - `task-board q 'get(STORY-260218-abc) { full }'`\n  - `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n  - `task-board m 'set_details(TASK-260218-def, scope=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n  - `task-board m 'link(TASK-260218-def, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc)'`\n- Each Task/Bug should be atomic: one clear specialist deliverable with verifiable AC and task-specific checklist items.\n- Spawn `solution-architect` only when detailing is blocked on a genuine architecture decision, such as a cross-module contract or system-design tradeoff. The Orchestrator routes decomposition quality checks through that tracked decision task, for example `task-board spawn STORY-260218-abc --role solution-architect --background`; the child produces the decision, and the Orchestrator still applies it to the board inline. Settled-scope decomposition itself remains inline Orchestrator work.\n- After detailing, set the Story to `to-dev`, not `done`: `task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc, status=to-dev)'`. `done` means accepted implementation, not completed decomposition.\n- **Do NOT proceed to Phase Planning until all stories have tasks**\n- This ensures the plan shows the real scope of work\n\n---\n\n## Phase Planning\n\n- **Review cross-story dependencies:** sub-agents set dependencies within their story, but orchestrator must link dependencies BETWEEN stories\n- Add missing cross-story/cross-epic ordering through task-level `task-board m 'link(...)'` dependencies; `plan()` derives story/epic edges from the live task DAG without persisting container links\n- Run `task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` to get flat execution `waves` and the single-line `criticalPath` from the dependency graph\n- Use default JSON `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` when nested `phases` and element details are needed\n- Verify the query-model waves/phases make sense — adjust dependencies and rerun `q plan()` if needed\n- Agents never need to render a graph to plan: the JSON/compact query model is the source of phases/waves and the critical path\n- **Dump the plan to `.planning/`** with timestamp (see above)\n\n---\n\n## User Approval (MANDATORY)\n\n- **ALWAYS show the plan to the user and wait for explicit approval**\n- Display:\n  - Wave and critical-path summary from `task-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-XX, mode=children)'`\n  - Nested phase/task details from `task-board q 'plan(STORY-XX, mode=children)'`\n  - Summary of what will be built\n- Ask: \"Plan ready. Proceed?\" (or similar)\n- **DO NOT start execution until user confirms**\n- If user has concerns -> adjust plan, re-show, re-confirm\n\n---\n\n## Pre-Launch Checklist (MANDATORY)\n\nBefore spawning ANY implementation agent, the coordinator MUST verify:\n\n- [ ] **Effective provider resolved** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and `project_config().spawn.schedule.active_window`; an active UTC window replaces the base system, and explicit `--agent` must stay inside the effective set\n- [ ] **Agent/model/reasoning selected explicitly** — query the selected provider's local registry, pass `--model` for every spawn and a compatible `--reasoning-effort` for Codex or Qwen; Claude rejects reasoning and no provider inherits defaults\n- [ ] **Quality-per-token assessment made** — assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; select the strongest suitable pair rather than the highest pair by reflex\n- [ ] **Board delegation ceiling applied** — use `max` as the highest recommended board-managed Codex effort; `ultra` is explicit compatibility for model-internal delegation, while task-board owns tracked roles, `RUN-...`, dependencies, and reviewer routing\n- [ ] **Role selected** — use `--role developer` / `--role researcher` / etc. Role auto-handles: start status, DoD checklist, skill injection, agent naming\n- [ ] Story status is `to-dev` (not backlog!) — `task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260218-abc, status=to-dev)'`\n- [ ] Agent assignment planned — the fully explicit tracked spawn applies it only after selection and task validation pass\n- [ ] Prompt includes CONCRETE task IDs (e.g., `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`), NOT placeholders like `TASK-XXX`\n- [ ] First task ID in prompt matches the Phase 1 unblocked task\n\n**If you skip this checklist, agents WILL ignore task-board updates.**\n\n---\n\n## Execution via Sub-Agents\n\n- **The coordinator is a SUPERVISOR — it does NOT write code itself**\n- ALL implementation work (code, tests, docs updates) goes to sub-agents\n- **Choose the strongest suitable registered model deliberately for every child** — maximize quality per token: use stronger capability for genuinely complex analysis, architecture, implementation, or review; choose balanced, fast, or cost-sensitive registered options only when they can pass the same required quality gates. Never rely on model or reasoning inheritance.\n- **Prepare tasks before spawning:** ensure each task has clear README (title, description, scope, AC) and link any instructions/specs as precondition resources via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=precondition)'`, with task-scoped resource names\n- **Spawn explicitly:** `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` auto-composes prompt from task README + resources + role identity\n- **Agent naming:** automatic with roles for Claude, Codex, and Qwen children. Archetype is visible in the dashboard.\n- Spawn sub-agents in parallel for independent stories (same phase)\n- Each sub-agent:\n  - Works on its story/task scope\n  - Writes code AND tests\n  - Updates task statuses as it progresses\n  - Saves work products as outcome resources via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Wait for Phase N to complete before starting Phase N+1\n- Even small tasks go to sub-agents — the coordinator only plans, delegates, monitors, and routes child outcomes\n\n---\n\n## Supervision\n\n- Monitor progress: `task-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'`\n- The coordinator owns the complete producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer loop through accepted `done`; reaching `to-review` never ends orchestration.\n- When a producer reaches `to-review`, spawn a reviewer child: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.\n- Review is delegated child work. The coordinator watches the `RUN-...` handle, reads the reviewer outcome resource, and routes from that verdict.\n- If the reviewer requests changes, preserve the verdict as context, return the task to `to-dev` or `analysis`, and spawn the right producer.\n- When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the task is actually `done`.\n- If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Never hand recoverable work to the human.\n- Never ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only at an explicit approval boundary.\n- Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or an unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision that cannot be resolved autonomously. Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\n- Never run tests, check code quality, validate integration, review docs, or produce a final verdict inline unless the human explicitly requests inline execution.\n\n---\n\n## Board Hygiene\n\n- Keep the board in sync with reality at all times\n- Move tasks through statuses as work progresses: `to-dev` -> `development` -> `to-review` -> `reviewing` -> `done`\n- Close stories when all tasks are done\n- Close epics when all stories are done\n- Run `task-board validate` periodically (local mode only)\n\n---\n\n## Plan Command Reference\n\nCanonical planning API:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q 'plan()'                                         # whole-project JSON phases + criticalPath\ntask-board q 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'                   # nested JSON phases + criticalPath\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'  # flat waves + single-line criticalPath\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'   # task/bug dependency closure\n```\n\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade over the same planning model:\n\n```bash\n# Full project plan (epic-level)\ntask-board plan\n\n# Epic plan (story-level)\ntask-board plan EPIC-01\n\n# Story plan (task-level)\ntask-board plan STORY-05\n\n# Save to file\ntask-board plan --save\ntask-board plan EPIC-01 --save\n\n# Specific phase\ntask-board plan --phase 2\n```\n\nThis facade is for presentation and `.planning/` persistence. Agents use `q plan()` for phases/waves and the critical path. Out-of-scope active blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error instead of producing phases that silently drop the edge.\n\n---\n\n## Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)\n\nRender a Graphviz artifact only when a human explicitly requests a graph. Rendering is never required for planning or execution: agents already get phases/waves and the critical path from the JSON/compact `q plan()` model.\n\n```bash\n# Hierarchy layout (default)\ntask-board plan --render\ntask-board plan EPIC-01 --render --format png\n\n# Phases layout\ntask-board plan --render --layout phases\ntask-board plan EPIC-01 --render --layout phases --format png\n\n# Render only active elements (exclude done/closed)\ntask-board plan --render --active\ntask-board plan EPIC-01 --render --active --format png\ntask-board plan --render --layout phases --active\n\n# Open the rendered file in the OS default viewer (explicit user request only)\ntask-board plan EPIC-01 --render --open\n```\n\n**`--active` flag:** Filters out `done` and `closed` elements from rendered graphs, showing only `open`, `progress`, and `blocked` elements. Works with both `--layout hierarchy` (default) and `--layout phases`. Useful during execution to focus on remaining work without visual clutter from completed items.\n\nRendered graphs go to a local system temp directory under `task-board-plan/`. `--render` writes the artifact and prints its path without opening a viewer; add `--open` only when the human explicitly asks to see the graph.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
